  {
    "episode_id": 95626,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95626.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "it or not, the biggest obstacles we're facing now are not a shortage of great science, not a shortage of great entrepreneurs, not a shortage of great investors. It's a threat of really bad policy. And what I mean by that is that, as we all know, some of these drugs can be expensive. We're an industry that fails 90% of the time. And it comes to Alzheimer's, we fail 100% of the time. And yet we still spend billions and billions of dollars trying to succeed. So when one of these companies and one of these projects clinical trials succeeds, It can often be expensive, at least, until it expires. And that means that people who have high deductibles on their insurance plan, and this is true of Medicare as well, they sometimes can't afford these drugs. And so politicians, understandably, I used to be one, want to do something about it, but they have to be very careful. And we think that the bad things that are being proposed are things that would impose government price controls, which would sort innovation. What we think they should do is pass legislation that would put a cap on what people have to pay out of their own pockets because we think this is a moral question. No one who needs medicine should ever be without it because they can't afford what's required to come from their pockets.",
